    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "txt_0885",
      "reading_order": 1046,
      "section": "10.3  ECG Processing",
      "bbox": {
        "l": 147.75,
        "t": 678.00375,
        "r": 537.6735374999987,
        "b": 624.74625
      },
      "content": "The  measurement  of  the  skin  surfaces  electrocardiogram  is  based  on  the  electrical signals  on  the  skin  surface,  produced  as  the  heart  muscle  contracts  and  relaxes.  The signals are detected by electrodes placed on the patient body. The information on heart activity carried by these signals varies with the placing of the electrodes.",
      "section_id": "sec_10_3",
      "parent_section_id": "sec_6",
      "section_level": 2,
      "section_path": [
        {
          "id": "sec_6",
          "title": "6. Turn monitor off."
        },
        {
          "id": "sec_10_3",
          "title": "10.3  ECG Processing"
        }
      ],
      "page": 94,
      "chunk_hint": "section_start",
      "semantic_role": "overview",
      "source": "pdf_text"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "txt_0886",
      "reading_order": 1047,
      "section": "10.3  ECG Processing",
      "bbox": {
        "l": 147.74887499999988,
        "t": 610.50375,
        "r": 539.0619000000002,
        "b": 570.74625
      },
      "content": "The technique used in ECG senses the varying potential difference between two points at the skin surface which respond to the chemical actions of the muscular activity of the heart.",
      "section_id": "sec_10_3",
      "parent_section_id": "sec_6",
      "section_level": 2,
      "section_path": [
        {
          "id": "sec_6",
          "title": "6. Turn monitor off."
        },
        {
          "id": "sec_10_3",
          "title": "10.3  ECG Processing"
        }
      ],
      "page": 94,
      "chunk_hint": "continue",
      "semantic_role": "description",
      "source": "pdf_text"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "txt_0887",
      "reading_order": 1048,
      "section": "10.3  ECG Processing",
      "bbox": {
        "l": 147.7443749999998,
        "t": 556.50375,
        "r": 539.0602124999998,
        "b": 476.24625
      },
      "content": "Three electrodes are attached  to the patient's right arm (RA), left arm (LA) and left leg (LL). The varying potentials at these locations are cable-connected to the ECG circuit inputs where they are conditioned, and the difference of potential between two selected leads  is  digitized  before  transmitting  through  opto-isolators  to  the  processor.  The processor-installed algorithms operate on the signals to develop drivers for the graphic display and to compute the heart rate in beats per minute (bpm).",
      "section_id": "sec_10_3",
      "parent_section_id": "sec_6",
      "section_level": 2,
      "section_path": [
        {
          "id": "sec_6",
          "title": "6. Turn monitor off."
        },
        {
          "id": "sec_10_3",
          "title": "10.3  ECG Processing"
        }
      ],
      "page": 94,
      "chunk_hint": "continue",
      "semantic_role": "description",
      "source": "pdf_text"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "txt_0888",
      "reading_order": 1049,
      "section": "10.3  ECG Processing",
      "bbox": {
        "l": 147.74549999999988,
        "t": 462.00375,
        "r": 539.0576249999999,
        "b": 435.74625
      },
      "content": "In  addition  to  the  acquisition  of  the  QRS  waveform  complex,  the  ECG  input  and subsequent signal processing computing circuitry perform a number of other functions:",
      "section_id": "sec_10_3",
      "parent_section_id": "sec_6",
      "section_level": 2,
      "section_path": [
        {
          "id": "sec_6",
          "title": "6. Turn monitor off."
        },
        {
          "id": "sec_10_3",
          "title": "10.3  ECG Processing"
        }
      ],
      "page": 94,
      "chunk_hint": "continue",
      "semantic_role": "description",
      "source": "pdf_text"
    },
    {
